    "description": "Efficient Request Queue observability dashboard. Panels are organized around capacity, latency, queueing, concurrency, reliability, LLM inference, streaming, and fairness questions.",
    "editable": true,
    "fiscalYearStartMonth": 0,
    "graphTooltip": 1,
    "id": null,
    "links": [],
    "liveNow": false,
    "panels": [
        {
            "id": 1,
            "type": "text",
            "title": "How to read this dashboard",
            "gridPos": {
                "x": 0,
                "y": 0,
                "w": 24,
                "h": 6
            },
            "options": {
                "mode": "markdown",
                "content": "### Dashboard questions and answers\n\n| Question | Dashboard signal |\n|---|---|\n| **Q1. How much work can the system successfully process?** | Panels 2, 3, and 21: accepted and successful jobs/sec. |\n| **Q2. How long do jobs wait for scheduler service?** | Panels 7 and 17: queue-wait percentiles. |\n| **Q3. How much concurrent work can workers sustain?** | Panels 5 and 9: worker in-flight jobs. |\n| **Q4. How often does it fail, retry, and recover?** | Panels 4, 11, 12, and 22: terminal outcomes, retries by reason, and recoveries by result. |\n| **Q5. Accepted job to response delivery latency?** | Panel 6: end-to-end percentiles. |\n| **Q6. Where is latency spent?** | Panel 17: admission, queue, processing, TTFT, generation, and stream p95s. Stages are diagnostic and not necessarily additive. |\n| **Q7. How quickly does inference produce output?** | Panel 13: time-to-first-token percentiles. |\n| **Q8. How long does model generation take?** | Panel 14: generation-duration percentiles. Token-volume metrics are not implemented. |\n| **Q9. How many users compete for scheduler capacity?** | Active-user metric is not implemented; use load-test harness context. |\n| **Q10. Is scheduler service fair and starvation-free?** | Panel 18: validate claim order, per-user wait/service share, scheduling gap, and starvation in the load-test harness. |\n\nHTTP panels 19, 20, and 24 use the **Route** and **Method** filters; panel 25 breaks traffic down by status class.\n\n> During load tests, increase offered RPS/concurrency and watch queue-wait and end-to-end latency, worker saturation, and retry/failure amplification."
            }
        },
        {
